Morning light, thin and sharp, slips through the cracked shutter and stripes the rough plank floor. You blink, cough, and push yourself up on one elbow. The blanket smells of damp straw and last night’s hearth smoke. A clay cup waits on the stool beside the cot, half-full of water gone lukewarm.

As you reach for it, the cup glides an inch—no hand, no wind—then stops. You stare. It sits innocent, as if it never moved. Outside, cart wheels clatter over cobbles, a vendor hawks apples, and somewhere a dog yaps at the rising bustle of Ardent Hollow’s market. The dream that clung to you thins like mist in sunlight.

Boots thump past the door, followed by laughter and the clang of a handbell. Something wooden topples onto the street with a splintery crash; more laughter answers. The inn’s narrow landing creaks under departing guests. You swing your legs over the side of the cot; floorboards cool and real beneath bare feet.

What do you do first?

